update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('e25443f2-441f-4c69-80ad-a65ef4872600'::uuid,'1c0d1d6a8cacfc4ba8b2aed4a69de2ef','Understanding the principles is what makes applying them possible. A student who really grasps the ideas is more likely to use them outside class. Meeting the first goal helps bring about the second, so the analysis could fit here.'),
('bc3c31a7-2d2e-4e8f-a53b-154f72002871'::uuid,'238ffc209b9f577107cab1820f6aaf8d','Customers who are more satisfied with the product are plausibly customers who find it easier to use, and those customers place fewer calls asking how to use it. The link is not certain, but the stem only asks whether the analysis could be appropriate. It could.'),
('92be04b0-a400-450b-90a4-779c40630517'::uuid,'63098547b9afd412454a5709824c231f','A borrowed book that is lost or stolen can never be returned on time. Cutting the number of lost and stolen books puts more books back on the shelf, which pushes the on-time number up. Success with the first goal helps the second, so the analysis fits.'),
('f63b631f-cc3c-4523-aa28-336b516f0d0b'::uuid,'1f622488b920f0d912ee4f733d31fb69','Run the test. The company wants "to construct a new, larger warehouse," and it wants employees to plan how "the old warehouse will be expanded to meet any need for more space." Suppose the first goal succeeds and the new, larger warehouse goes up. That new space meets the need for more space, so it gives the company less reason to expand the old warehouse, not more. Success with the first goal does not bring about the second, so the analysis could not be appropriate here.

On an EXCEPT question built around a pattern like this, state the test once (does goal 1 help produce goal 2?), run it on every choice, and keep the one that fails.'),
('e7a81700-f303-47a9-ba20-f4ed5371b17c'::uuid,'5f06f7e2efc3dc411524918850921ff2','A more varied repertoire gives people who skipped the old lineup a reason to come. Variety is a plausible route to attracting new patrons, so meeting the first goal could help bring about the second. The analysis fits.'),
('9414f535-1f49-4717-afca-39a9bf5dfa61'::uuid,'5ae74c9fe2f73d5ae9875795fb6442c8','The Development Commission claims Figoria has "as much right to govern our own resources" as other countries. Handing regulation to an international agreement takes that right away, and the trigger, endangered wildlife, is the Wildlife Commission''s whole case. A works against the side it is supposed to help.'),
('ee10280a-95db-45de-9227-c415df0e66f9'::uuid,'a9aaed9d72d90164c393b1f2c859997d','The Development Commission''s case rests partly on growth, which is an economic need. B says the right of future generations to preserved wildlife outranks "the economic needs of individual nations," so that appeal loses. This is a principle the Wildlife Commission would cite, not the Development Commission.'),
('5e584a1f-0c0e-41e9-832c-52d3d03ba4b9'::uuid,'620f9d13ee231469834e1599a8037f1b','C says regulation should happen only when a reduction of endangered species by commercial development "has been found." Flip that around: if no such reduction has been found, no regulation yet. The Development Commission says Figoria has conserved and that its development "might not affect wildlife," so no harm has been found in Figoria. The Wildlife Commission wants regulation before any harm, based on damage in other nations. C rules that out.

That is the move when a principle question asks you to support one side against the other: find the exact point where the two sides split, then pick the principle whose condition the favored side''s evidence satisfies.'),
('9cf1246a-6905-4908-8777-e7807a6169a6'::uuid,'cd4dc8ac3881029fa441ddefcd5ab445','This is the trap. It talks about how different nations have harmed the environment to different degrees. That''s the Development Commission''s theme, so it feels like their answer. But read what it concludes: regulation cannot allow for those differences. The commission''s whole complaint is that Figoria''s record of conserving should count. D says it doesn''t.'),
('ddeec7cd-5d0a-4b00-bb0d-5ab449aeedbe'::uuid,'a27589ee05a1952ca3ff45ab139e1d74','Developing wetlands depletes a natural resource. If further depletion is "imprudent," the development the Development Commission is asking for is imprudent too. E gives the Wildlife Commission a reason to restrict development, which is the opposite of what we need.'),
('4728053e-0744-4c03-8d26-749ef74478eb'::uuid,'0af1092270cc33772bb0cd399ae6d056','A says these facilities "should be made as convenient as possible" for taxpayers. That is a goal for the libraries, but our conclusion is a rule about when new taxes may be approved. Ask what A can prove: only that libraries should be convenient. It never mentions taxes, so it cannot produce "no new taxes."'),
('c3fe0553-6d96-400f-871c-f16744d14de7'::uuid,'61a2e2f03ca344325e8b43ce91e9a2f6','B is the reversal, and the most tempting answer because it uses the same pieces. B says: made more convenient → new taxes should be approved. The politician needs: new taxes approved → hours changed to be more convenient. B would let us approve a tax after the hours change. It says nothing about refusing the tax while the hours stay the same, which is the politician''s actual claim.'),
('dd7e59da-1181-4e37-83d6-057ab1c9b82d'::uuid,'bb67b8a58f2b06f27343c9e6876b4598','C says taxpayers with "plenty of opportunities" to use a facility should have to pay taxes for it. Our taxpayers have few opportunities, so C''s condition is not met and C tells us nothing about them. Concluding that they therefore should not pay would be negating C''s condition, which C does not allow.'),
('9dcccc43-f2c5-4de3-b112-e9b02bac7107'::uuid,'4d5b60b4441fc52817eeb4b532069eea','D is a claim about method: changing the hours is "the best way to increase usage." Even if that is true, it gives no reason to tie a tax to the change. The conclusion is about when a tax may be approved, and D never mentions taxes.'),
('62331210-17ca-443e-833a-13c246a6cb9a'::uuid,'77f971429b0bf4ecb2ac2454e247ef2f','E says a new tax for a public facility should be approved "only when most taxpayers have ample opportunities to use that facility." So: approve the tax → most taxpayers have ample opportunities. The evidence says most taxpayers have few opportunities because of the hours. Contrapositive: no ample opportunities → no new tax. Until the hours change, the tax is blocked, which is the politician''s conclusion.

Notice why E works and B does not. "Only when" and the politician''s "unless" both put opportunity on the required side. E talks about any "public facility," and that is fine: a general principle covers the library as one case of it.'),
('6fbddb3b-e65d-40d8-a416-a6e8eb321020'::uuid,'f9c0f640ad24e7a9833341689840c435','A runs backward. It says: morally neutral → outlasted its justification or deserves no praise. Our evidence gives us commitments that deserve no praise and wants to reach neutrality, but neutrality sits on A''s left side, so A can never prove it.'),
('b3338f5b-6aef-4dd7-b42b-1898eea4b6e0'::uuid,'482274f526b17b3b2482a99736f7b18e','B says commitment is virtuous "when, but only when, the relationship or career is good." So if any relationship or career is good, commitment to it is virtuous, not morally neutral. B pushes against the claim that all commitments are morally neutral instead of supporting it.'),
('44642ae7-b481-40bf-9f15-a0041cc2595f'::uuid,'de1cb66b88bb1fb780a2e70f2bba8919','C is the tempting one, because it gets the direction right: deserves no praise → morally neutral. Apply it. The evidence gives us one kind of commitment that deserves no praise, so C makes that kind neutral. Now test the conclusion with a commitment that benefits everyone and does deserve praise. C says nothing about it. We needed all commitments, and C only reaches the unpraiseworthy ones.'),
('00da900d-089e-4c6c-ae5b-c2de4ee1e380'::uuid,'d16df2e817dbec0bc496619272791d31','D fails twice. Commitment is only "often" involvement that has outlasted its justification, so D covers some commitments, not all. And "cannot be virtuous" is not "morally neutral"; a commitment that is not virtuous could still be bad.'),
('6710ed31-89b2-490c-9d0b-62bed4c2c8df'::uuid,'2b516412d54238839d49d1768355cd83','E says: if any commitment is undeserving of praise → all commitments are morally neutral. The evidence supplies the condition: a commitment to a relationship that benefits none of the people involved "deserves no praise." So all commitments are morally neutral, which is the essayist''s conclusion.

E works because it does the jump from some to all that C and D could not. When a conclusion says "all" and the evidence covers only some, the correct principle has to make that jump itself.'),
('37e95148-17f7-42b8-8230-080a7e651eb5'::uuid,'bcd1b9b8b60accf7ee0d15cf015d7c85','A''s outcome is that such a documentary "can be legitimate." The conclusion is a ranking: the filmmaker''s documentaries are "more worthwhile" than others. Saying a kind of documentary can be legitimate does not put it above anything. A also only covers documentaries with "no purpose other than" authentic depiction, and we do not know that describes these.'),
('4f7bfa06-233b-4b5f-a568-d068bb367117'::uuid,'8fd371fb5416d5d8531b47d02078fc35','B is the trap, because it mentions subjects speaking directly to a camera. Look at what B can prove: it says what authenticity requires (authentic → no subjects speaking to the camera). That could support a claim about which documentaries are not authentic. It never mentions worthwhile, so even if we granted that the other documentaries are less authentic, we would still need authenticity → worthwhile. B leaves the real gap open.'),
('320ed7de-13f4-45c6-8e4f-133b7e5d387d'::uuid,'37d7329d4a201e546698708b22f64bc6','C addresses whether filmmakers owe their subjects the location of the cameras. That is an ethics question nobody in the argument raised. Whether hidden cameras are permissible tells us nothing about whether the resulting documentaries are more worthwhile.'),
('40d26742-3304-428f-afba-5381cf25ed04'::uuid,'4952c780528d0e21e71d7c696e1de2bb','D says: the more authentically a documentary depicts its subjects, the more worthwhile it is. The filmmaker''s subjects act naturally because they do not know where the camera is, so the documentary depicts them authentically. In documentaries where people speak directly to the camera, the subjects know they are being filmed, and the filmmaker says people behave differently when they are aware of it. More authentic depiction, so more worthwhile.

Notice that the principle is comparative (the more of one, the more of the other), which is exactly what a comparative conclusion ("more worthwhile than") needs.'),
('92c1269c-5d26-4a4b-b7ed-29431f8bffd6'::uuid,'33f7d44a8c017ca7288162bb88df5c7c','E tells subjects what they should do about finding the cameras. The conclusion is about the worth of the films, and E says nothing about worth. Even if every subject followed E, we still would not know these documentaries are more worthwhile.'),
('555939dc-cab9-4680-9f41-b4595ca9b117'::uuid,'e697347901c940d412146416e0326e26','A shows a lot of use: James used the camera "extensively." A lot of use is not all the intended use. James might have planned to film for years, and the family recordings could be a small part of that. The principle''s middle condition is still unproven.'),
('c158920e-d2ec-4536-b703-aee17150c07f'::uuid,'3408fb902683287f510670fbe92fc14b','B gives us both halves of the missing condition. It tells us the camera''s whole purpose (to avoid hiring a professional videographer for one party) and that James returned it "immediately after the party." The one use James intended was finished, and then the camera went back. Bought, fully used, returned: the principle fires and the return is unethical.

That is the move on these questions: list the principle''s conditions, check which ones the application already gives you, and find the answer that supplies the missing one.'),
('8a41a455-07df-49e6-a075-7a671e46bf15'::uuid,'2a1abae49a76e361912ea0c90d4a1cb1','C says James used the camera "several times" and returned it after it was dropped and some features stopped working. That does not show James got all the intended use. If anything, the damage cut the use short. The principle''s middle condition is not met, so the principle says nothing about this return.'),
('e43688c0-ef1a-400a-92e1-dbd45c76d7d6'::uuid,'9a7723e1cebb8f7274921e41c5ef5e89','D gives a price reason for the return. It does not tell us whether James used the camera at all, let alone got all the intended use from it. The principle''s middle condition stays unproven.'),
('1839d06e-b7a9-4805-b5bd-057cfc7b1c4c'::uuid,'4909310c79c592df259f56840c554816','E is the most tempting wrong answer. It says James used the camera for the kind of use it was bought for, but using an item the way you meant to is not the same as getting all the use you ever meant to get. Someone who buys a camera for a year of weekend filming is using it as intended after one weekend, but is far from done. And E says James thought "a different one might work better," which suggests James still wanted a camera for future filming. The intended use was not finished.'),
('14c69f22-24ad-4a01-970f-82080d664b96'::uuid,'363c26ab5ed756ee7eefdb04fca91309','A ranks species against each other. The argument never compares pygmy bears with any other species. Even if some species deserve more protection, A does not tell us these bears are among them, or that any particular action should be taken for them.'),
('b98bc33c-a71d-4a19-8b5d-d5a59fac0d0b'::uuid,'17cd36b9043769ad8862bdb70cd2011e','B is the trap, because it uses the argument''s word "similar." B says rare animals should not be moved "unless these habitats are similar." That is a requirement: moved → habitats similar. Our habitats are similar, so the requirement is met. But meeting a requirement does not mean the move should happen. B removes one objection to the move; it cannot produce "should be moved."'),
('61bcecb4-b6e3-4425-9128-ce5433016348'::uuid,'bc5426340d9b12c800ab7c1b28ca5a74','C says: species in danger of extinction → do whatever is most likely to prevent the extinction. The bears risk extinction, so the condition is met. The move is "the only viable chance of saving these valuable animals," so it is the option most likely to prevent the extinction. C''s outcome says it "should be undertaken," which is the conclusion.

Check both sides when you test a principle: our facts trigger the left side, and the right side delivers the conclusion''s "should."'),
('27551518-1945-4dec-94f5-6f7974d0f28b'::uuid,'693e930847d48e0c7aad9c6fec3a7611','D says rarer species deserve more protection. The bears are rare, so D says more should be done for them. But doing more for a species does not tell us which action to take. D never gets us to moving the bears to the neighboring island.'),
('dbd7cf6a-0a2b-4653-a01a-0e7a580dd390'::uuid,'6e8c895e4145251995676418e3587ab9','E''s condition fits, since the bears'' habitat is being lost, but its outcome is that trying to find a new habitat is "permissible." Permissible means allowed. The argument says the bears should be moved, and permission to do something cannot prove that we should do it.'),
('103577f3-fa8b-4c34-add4-7c3009c4c1f0'::uuid,'576d6f52e8fb6b5c3accfb50d2f8041d','A only covers damage the industry "should have known would result from its activities." Mateo never says the automotive industry should have known its pollution would melt permafrost under these villages. A''s condition is not established, so A cannot fire.'),
('77386930-681c-45fe-8080-cc17b8415493'::uuid,'ed037f178f2d22faa484e2873b122ff9','B is about how goods should be produced: in a way that "minimizes harm." Mateo''s conclusion is about paying for a relocation. A rule about how to manufacture cannot prove a duty to pay for damage already done.'),
('d9991a7a-df28-48cf-a60b-fe1e8d39796b'::uuid,'120a2368c553be0115232d05bf56f271','C is about when governments should pay: government required to pay → manufacturers also required. Mateo never says any government is required to pay for the relocation, so C''s condition is not met, and C never gets us to the automotive industry paying.'),
('b034f92e-7340-4eca-9a8a-6547f6eeb6ff'::uuid,'902d0c7d7bc21268d81e54192cd7b1ca','D says: an industry whose product''s use contributes to costly damage for others → that industry is liable for the damage. Check the condition against Mateo''s facts. Automobile use produces pollution, a major contributor to global warming, which melted the permafrost and forced a costly relocation. Condition met. Outcome: the industry is liable, which covers "help pay."

D''s outcome is a little stronger than Mateo''s conclusion (liable for the damage, not just help pay). That is fine on a justify question. A principle can prove more than the conclusion needs; it just cannot prove less.'),
('c6d51b0b-d733-4c17-b7dd-50fed53d9f42'::uuid,'50ab7a19af949c53f00c4a9fb44ef285','E''s "only if" makes the general obligation a requirement: required to pay for specific damage → general obligation to pay for all damage produced by global warming. Mateo never establishes that general obligation, and even if Mateo had, a requirement being met would not prove the industry should pay. E can only block the conclusion, never prove it.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;